.TH COMPARTMENT-USER 1 "June 2026" "compartment 1.3.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
compartment-user \- userspace process isolation with Landlock, seccomp, and environment sanitization
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B compartment-user
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B compartment-user
applies kernel-enforced restrictions to a process before executing it.
No root required. Zero dependencies.
.PP
Three of the mechanisms are enforced by the kernel. Once set they are
inherited across
.BR fork (2)
and
.BR execve (2)
and cannot be removed by the sandboxed process or any of its descendants:
.IP \(bu 4
.B PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS
\- prevents privilege escalation via setuid binaries.
.IP \(bu
.B Landlock
\- filesystem path restrictions (read-only system paths, writable working directory).
.IP \(bu
.B seccomp BPF
\- blocks dangerous syscalls (ptrace, mount, bpf, io_uring, etc.).
.PP
The rest is applied once, immediately before
.BR execve (2),
and is
.B not
a kernel restriction:
.IP \(bu 4
.B Environment sanitization
\- strips LD_PRELOAD, cloud credentials, SSH agent socket, etc. from the
environment handed to the command.
A sandboxed process may re-export any of these variables for its own
children, and the loader will honour them; nothing in the kernel prevents
that.
Durable environment policy needs
.BR compartment-bpf .
.IP \(bu
.B Working directory
and
.B file-descriptor cleanup
\- both are one-time actions, not ongoing restrictions.
.IP \(bu
.B Audit logging
\- a record, not a restriction. File-per-day log with PPID chain and
user identity.
.PP
The actual order at run time is: audit log opened, preflight check,
.BR PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS ,
environment sanitization, Landlock, seccomp,
.BR chdir (2),
hardening,
.BR execve (2).
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Profiles
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " NAME|FILE"
Load a security profile.
Built-in profiles:
.BR ai-agent " (default),"
.BR strict ", "
.BR none .
File search order:
.IR /etc/compartment/NAME.conf ,
then
.I ~/.config/compartment/NAME.conf
but only with
.BR \-\-user\-profiles .
An explicit file path (containing \fB/\fR) loads directly.
Every profile file must be a regular file owned by root or by the real uid of
the caller, must not be group- or world-writable, and its containing directory
must satisfy the same conditions.
A profile that exists but does not parse is fatal; the built-in is used only
when no file was found.
.TP
.B \-\-user\-profiles
Also search
.I ~/.config/compartment/NAME.conf
for profiles.
Off by default: the sandboxed process usually has write access to
.IR $HOME ,
so a profile found there could switch its own sandbox off.
Never honoured in shell-replacement mode.
.SS "Filesystem (Landlock)"
.PP
The built-in profiles add
.I $HOME
as a read-write-execute Landlock root.
.I $HOME
must therefore be an absolute path, must not be
.IR / ,
and must name an existing directory owned by the real uid of the caller;
compartment-user refuses to start otherwise.
The same applies to
.I $HOME
expanded inside a profile value.
.PP
A rule may name a directory or a single file.
A rule that names a file carries only the file-level rights
(execute, read, write, truncate, device ioctl); the directory-only rights
are dropped, which is what makes
.B \-\-rw
.I /dev/null
and
.B \-\-exec
.I /usr/bin/psql
meaningful.
.PP
A rule for a path that does not exist is a
.B fatal error:
it would grant nothing, and a policy that believes otherwise is worse than
one that refuses to start.
Append
.B ?
to the path to make the rule optional
.RI ( "\-\-ro /lib32?" ):
it is then skipped, with a note under
.BR \-\-verbose ,
when the path is absent.
.PP
Landlock is
.B additive:
the rights of every rule matching an ancestor of the path being opened are
unioned, so a narrower rule never restricts a wider one.
A
.B ro
rule whose path lies inside a
.B rw
or
.B rwx
rule cannot take anything away and is refused; split the writable rules
instead.
An
.B exec
rule inside a
.B rw
rule is allowed, because it genuinely adds a right.
.TP
.BI \-\-ro " PATH"
Grant read-only + execute access to \fIPATH\fR and everything beneath it.
.TP
.BI \-\-rw " PATH"
Grant read-write access to \fIPATH\fR.
Execute is deliberately
.B not
granted: compartment-user enforces W^X, so a directory that a program can
write to is never a directory it can execute from.
Use
.B \-\-exec
(or
.BR \-\-ro )
for execute access, and note that a path listed both \fB\-\-rw\fR and
\fB\-\-exec\fR ends up read-write-execute, because Landlock rules are
additive.
.TP
.BI \-\-exec " PATH"
Grant read + execute access to \fIPATH\fR.
On a directory this is the same set of rights as
.BR \-\-ro .
On a
.I file
it is a per-binary execute grant: a policy that names individual files with
.B \-\-exec
and grants execute on no directory is an allow-list of binaries.
Two caveats decide whether that works.
The dynamic loader must be listed as well, because
.BR execve (2)
opens the ELF interpreter with
.B FMODE_EXEC
and Landlock checks the execute right on it.
Shared libraries must
.I not
be: the loader opens them read-only and Landlock has no mmap hook, so read
access to the library directory is enough \(em and
.B \-\-rw
is the more precise choice there, since
.B \-\-ro
grants execute on everything beneath it.
The grant keys on the file, so a busybox-style multi-call binary cannot be
split into applets, and a shell builtin is not an
.BR execve (2)
at all.
.TP
.BI \-\-workdir " PATH"
Set working directory. It is added as a path rule in its own right \(em
read, write and execute, the same rights the built-in profile gives
.IR $HOME ,
not the W^X rights of
.BR \-\-rw .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-landlock
Disable Landlock filesystem restrictions.
.SS "Network (Landlock, ABI v4 / Linux 6.7+)"
.PP
TCP only.
These rules cover
.BR bind (2)
and
.BR connect (2)
on TCP and nothing else: no UDP, no unix sockets, no netlink, no raw
sockets, no
.BR listen / accept
granularity, and no per-address rules \(em a rule for port 443 allows port
443 on every address, IPv4 and IPv6 alike.
They are an allow-list; there is no way to express \(lqeverything except
port N\(rq.
Below Landlock ABI v4 compartment-user prints a warning and the port policy
is
.B not
active, while the filesystem rules still are.
.TP
.BI \-\-net\-bind " PORT"
Allow
.BR bind (2)
on TCP \fIPORT\fR. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-net\-connect " PORT"
Allow
.BR connect (2)
to TCP \fIPORT\fR. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-net\-deny
Handle TCP bind and connect and refuse every port not named above.
Without this flag and without any
.B \-\-net\-*
rule the network is not restricted at all, which is the default.
Note that naming only connect rules still leaves
.BR bind (2)
unrestricted, while
.B \-\-net\-deny
refuses both \(em including the explicit
.BR bind (2)
on port 0 that some clients make before connecting.
.SS "Syscalls (seccomp BPF)"
.TP
.BI \-\-block " SYSCALL"
Block a syscall by name or number. Repeatable.
Up to 256 entries; the built-in
.B ai-agent
profile already uses 43 of them, and overflowing the limit is a fatal
error rather than a silent truncation.
.TP
.BI \-\-allow " SYSCALL"
Switch seccomp to allow-list mode and permit
.IR SYSCALL .
Everything not named by an
.B \-\-allow
flag or an
.B allow
profile directive returns
.BR EPERM .
Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-seccomp
Disable seccomp filtering.
.PP
What a denied syscall does is chosen by the
.B seccomp\-default
profile directive:
.B errno
(the default,
.BR EPERM ),
.B kill
.RB ( SECCOMP_RET_KILL_PROCESS ,
a
.B SIGSYS
the process cannot mishandle) or
.B log
(permit and record, for working out what a policy needs).
.B errno
remains the default for compatibility; it is the weakest of the three,
because a program that does not check return values corrupts its own logic
instead of dying, and every denial returns cleanly enough for an attacker to
probe the filter one call at a time.
.SS Environment
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-allow " VAR"
Switch environment handling to allow-list mode and keep
.IR VAR .
Everything not named by an
.B \-\-env\-allow
flag or an
.B env-allow
profile directive is removed. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-deny " VAR"
Strip an environment variable. Repeatable.
A trailing \fB*\fR makes the entry a prefix match, so
.B \-\-env\-deny
.I LD_*
strips the whole loader family.
The same applies to
.BR \-\-env\-allow .
.PP
The built-in profile strips the loader, shell-startup, interpreter and
git-helper families along with cloud, VCS and database credentials.
It deliberately does
.B not
strip the
.I *_API_KEY
variables an agent authenticates its model provider with: removing the
credential the sandboxed agent needs in order to start would defeat the
tool's main purpose.
Environment sanitization removes what the sandboxed process should not
have; it cannot protect a secret deliberately handed to it.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-env\-sanitize
Do not strip dangerous environment variables.
.SS General
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what restrictions would be applied without executing the command.
Implies
.BR \-\-verbose .
.TP
.BI \-\-dump\-profile " NAME"
Resolve
.I NAME
the way a real run would and print the effective policy as
.B .conf
syntax on standard output, then exit.
No command is needed.
Use it to generate a profile file that is guaranteed to match what the
binary enforces:
.RS
.nf

compartment-user \-\-dump\-profile ai\-agent | sudo tee /etc/compartment/my\-agent.conf

.fi
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Print actions to stderr, including the resolved profile and its source.
.TP
.B \-\-audit
Log events to stderr and to a file.
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-log " DIR"
Set audit log directory (implies \fB\-\-audit\fR).
The directory must be owned by the effective uid and must not be group- or
world-writable; it is opened with
.B O_DIRECTORY
and
.B O_NOFOLLOW
and the daily file is created relative to that descriptor, so neither the
directory nor the file may be a symlink.
If auditing was requested and cannot be set up safely,
.B compartment-user
refuses to run.
.PP
An operator-chosen directory that lies inside a granted
.B rw
or
.B rwx
path is reachable by the sandboxed process, which can then read and
rewrite the trail. Choose one that no path rule covers.
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-audit\-log ,
the default is chosen in this order:
.RS
.IP 1. 4
.IR /var/lib/compartment/audit/ UID ,
if an administrator provisioned it. It is used only when the parent
.I /var/lib/compartment/audit
is owned by root and is not group- or world-writable, and the per-uid
directory is a real directory owned by the caller's real uid with mode
0700. Provision it with:
.RS
.nf

install \-d \-m 0755 \-o root \-g root /var/lib/compartment/audit
install \-d \-m 0700 \-o USER /var/lib/compartment/audit/UID

.fi
.RE
.IP 2.
.IR /var/tmp/compartment\-audit\- UID ,
created mode 0700. Because
.I /var/tmp
is sticky and world-writable, another user can create that name first:
if the directory is found with a different owner or a mode other than
0700,
.B compartment-user
refuses to run rather than appending to it.
.RE
.PP
Running as root short-circuits both of those: the default is
.IR /var/log/compartment ,
created mode 0700, and neither step above is consulted.
.PP
Neither default is inside a path the built-in profiles grant for writing,
so the sandboxed process cannot rewrite its own audit trail. Note that
.I /var/lib
is granted
.B ro
by the built-in
.B ai-agent
profile, so a log kept there is readable — though not writable or
removable — from inside the sandbox;
.I /var/tmp
is covered by no rule at all and is therefore neither.
.TP
.B \-\-insecure
Allow execution when enforcement is degraded.
.B compartment-user
runs a pre-flight check before executing the command. If any check fails
(missing Landlock, unsupported filesystem, etc.), execution is refused
by default. This flag overrides the refusal and proceeds with reduced
protection, printing warnings to stderr.
When \fB\-\-audit\fR is also set, a
.B COMPARTMENT_DEGRADED
event is logged.
.B \-\-unsecure
is accepted as an alias.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Check kernel support (Landlock, seccomp, filesystem type) and exit.
Reports the Landlock ABI version, detects unsupported filesystems
(9p, NFS, CIFS), and verifies seccomp BPF availability.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print usage summary.
.SH PRE-FLIGHT CHECK
Before executing a command,
.B compartment-user
automatically validates that requested enforcement mechanisms are
available and functional:
.IP \(bu 2
.B PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS
\(em required for seccomp and privilege escalation prevention.
.IP \(bu
.B Landlock availability
\(em checks the kernel ABI version. Notes if ABI < 2 (no cross-dir rename restriction).
.IP \(bu
.B Filesystem type
\(em detects 9p (virtme-ng/QEMU), NFS, and CIFS filesystems where Landlock
cannot enforce path restrictions. Uses both
.BR statfs (2)
and
.I /proc/mounts
because 9p can proxy the underlying filesystem type through statfs.
.IP \(bu
.B seccomp BPF
\(em verifies the kernel accepts BPF filters.
.PP
If any check fails, execution is refused with suggestions:
.RS
.nf
compartment-user: REFUSING to execute \(em 1 preflight check failed.
  Options:
    --insecure      Run anyway with degraded enforcement
    --no-landlock   Disable Landlock (if filesystem unsupported)
    --verify        Show full system capabilities
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use \fB\-\-insecure\fR to proceed with degraded protection, or
\fB\-\-no-landlock\fR to skip filesystem restrictions entirely.
.SH SHELL REPLACEMENT MODE
When invoked via a symlink whose name is not
.IR compartment-user
(e.g., as \fI/bin/bash\fR, \fI/bin/zsh\fR, \fI/bin/tcsh\fR, or any other
shell), the program enters shell-replacement mode:
it applies the
.B ai-agent
profile and execs the real shell from the stash directory
(compile-time \fBREAL_SHELL_DIR\fR, or the \fBCOMPARTMENT_SHELL_DIR\fR
environment variable when that names a directory worth trusting).
The profile is read from
.I /etc/compartment/ai-agent.conf
or the compiled-in default;
.I $HOME
is never searched in this mode.
.PP
.B COMPARTMENT_SHELL_DIR
is caller-supplied, so it is honoured only when it is an absolute path
with no \fB..\fR component and both the directory and the shell binary
inside it are owned by root or by the real uid of the caller and are
neither group- nor world-writable.
Otherwise it is ignored with a warning on standard error and the
compile-time
.B REAL_SHELL_DIR
is used instead.
Either way the sandbox is applied before the exec: an accepted
.B COMPARTMENT_SHELL_DIR
changes which binary runs, never whether it is confined.
.PP
Shell-replacement mode performs the same preflight check, ambient
capability clearing,
.BR PR_SET_DUMPABLE (2)
reset and file-descriptor cleanup as the normal path.
.PP
.B "IMPORTANT:"
Shell-replacement mode
.B never blocks login.
If any enforcement mechanism fails (missing Landlock, unsupported
filesystem, etc.), the shell is still executed but with a
.B syslog
warning at
.B LOG_WARNING
level, including uid, pid, and ppid for audit trail:
.RS
.nf
compartment-user[bash]: INSECURE shell session (1 mechanism failed) uid=1000 pid=12345 ppid=12340
.fi
.RE
.PP
This ensures that replacing \fI/bin/bash\fR (or any shell) with a symlink
to compartment-user can never lock out users, while still providing
audit visibility when enforcement is degraded.
.PP
Build with a randomized stash path:
.RS
.nf
make hardened
.fi
.RE
.SH PROFILE FORMAT
Profiles use a simple line-oriented format shared with
.BR compartment-root (8):
.PP
.nf
.RS
# Filesystem.  A rule may name a directory or a file; a
# trailing '?' makes the rule optional instead of fatal.
ro /usr
ro /lib32?
rw /dev/null
exec /usr/bin/psql
rw $HOME

# Network (TCP only, Landlock ABI v4 / Linux 6.7+)
net-connect 5432
net-default deny

# Syscalls
block ptrace
block mount
seccomp-default errno

# Environment
env-deny LD_*

# Features
landlock on
seccomp on
no-new-privs on
env-sanitize on

# Inheritance
inherit ai-agent
.RE
.fi
.PP
Boolean values accept:
.BR on / off ,
.BR yes / no ,
.BR true / false ,
.BR 1 / 0
(case-insensitive). Unrecognized values are rejected.
.PP
Unknown directives produce a warning to help catch typos.
.SH DEFAULT DENY LIST (ai-agent profile)
The built-in
.B ai-agent
profile blocks these syscall families:
.PP
.nf
ptrace, process_vm_readv, process_vm_writev,
mount, umount2, pivot_root, chroot, unshare, setns,
mount_setattr, open_tree, move_mount,
fsopen, fsmount, fsconfig, fspick,
open_by_handle_at, name_to_handle_at,
reboot, kexec_load, kexec_file_load,
init_module, finit_module, delete_module,
keyctl, add_key, request_key,
bpf, userfaultfd, perf_event_open,
io_uring_setup, io_uring_enter, io_uring_register,
acct, swapon, swapoff,
settimeofday, clock_settime, clock_adjtime, adjtimex,
pidfd_getfd, and on x86-64 ioperm and iopl
.fi
.PP
Environment variables stripped include LD_PRELOAD, cloud credentials
(AWS, GCP, Azure), VCS tokens (GitHub, GitLab), SSH_AUTH_SOCK,
database passwords, and interpreter injection vectors (BASH_ENV,
NODE_OPTIONS, PYTHONSTARTUP, etc.).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Sandbox an AI agent with defaults:
.B compartment-user \-\- claude \-\-model claude-opus-4-6
.TP
Use a strict profile:
.B compartment-user \-\-profile strict \-\- codex \-\-full-auto
.TP
Custom filesystem rules:
.B compartment-user \-\-rw /data \-\-block ptrace \-\- ./my\-agent
.TP
Preview the policy:
.B compartment-user \-\-dry\-run \-\- claude
.TP
Read-only SSH client:
.B compartment-user \-\-profile ./examples/ssh.conf \-\- ssh user@host
.TP
Check kernel support:
.B compartment-user \-\-verify
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Command executed successfully under sandbox.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration error, missing command, or profile load failure.
.TP
.B 126
Reserved. Shell-replacement mode no longer refuses to run: a rejected
.B COMPARTMENT_SHELL_DIR
is a warning and a fall back to the compile-time stash directory, and an
enforcement failure is logged to syslog.
.TP
.B 127
Command not found or not executable under Landlock restrictions.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/compartment/*.conf
Per-user profile files.
Searched only when
.B \-\-user\-profiles
is given, and never in shell-replacement mode.
.TP
.I /etc/compartment/*.conf
System-wide profile files.
.TP
.IR /var/lib/compartment/audit/ UID /
Preferred audit log directory when an administrator has provisioned it
(one file per day).
.TP
.IR /var/tmp/compartment\-audit\- UID /
Fallback audit log directory.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Linux >= 5.13 (Landlock). seccomp BPF requires >= 3.17.
No external libraries.
